{
"slots": {
"question": [
{
"claim": "The paper investigates whether Democratic Party campaign advertisements labeling Donald Trump as a 'convicted criminal' cause stigmatizing spillover effects on Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records, and whether a counter-narrative featuring a formerly system-involved representative can mitigate these effects.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "To test for the possibility of stigmatizing language spillover, this preliminary study employs a survey experiment testing exposure to the Party’s advertisement, labeling Trump as a 'convicted criminal'... on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records and several dimensions of perceived representation.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Thus, this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats’ perceptions of people with criminal records, and to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well Democrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
"page": 4
}
]
}
],
"central_claim": [
{
"claim": "Exposure to Democratic Party advertisements labeling Trump as a 'convicted criminal' negatively impacts Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records, but this negative effect is largely ameliorated when participants are first exposed to a counter-narrative featuring the perspective of a formerly system-involved state representative.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Results suggest that exposure to the Trump advertisement with the 'convicted criminal' label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved perceived feelings of representation. Importantly, the effect of a counter-stereotype’s ability to mitigate these effects was also tested... Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly ameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with criminal records.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "First, there is clear evidence that the Trump advertisement caused decreases in positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records amongst Democrats, but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated, system-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the 'criminal' label, these effects were almost entirely ameliorated",
"page": 15
}
]
}
],
"evidence": [
{
"claim": "The study utilizes a survey experiment with 2,439 self-identified Democrats recruited via Prolific, randomly assigning them to three groups: a control group, a group exposed only to a Trump advertisement labeled 'convicted criminal,' and a group exposed to a counter-narrative about a formerly incarcerated state representative before seeing the same advertisement.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Thus, the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
"page": 4
},
{
"text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform... 2,500 participants were recruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025... The latter inclusion criteria is important, given the purpose of the study is to understand the impacts of Democratic Party advertising on feelings of being adequately represented... thus the study sample focused only on their constituents.",
"page": 8
}
]
}
],
"key_findings": [
{
"claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records compared to the control group.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Hypothesis 2A: Being presented with the Democratic party’s Trump advertisement alone, labeling him as a 'convicted criminal,' will significantly decrease (positive) perceptions of individuals with criminal records, on average, relative to not being exposed to this advertisement",
"page": 7
},
{
"text": "Both H2A and H2B were supported, namely, exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Presenting a counter-narrative about a formerly incarcerated legislator before the advertisement significantly increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records compared to seeing the advertisement alone.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Hypothesis 1B: Exposure to the counter-narrative first... will significantly increase (positive) perceptions of individuals with criminal records, on average, relative to exposure to the advertisement of Trump being labeled as a 'convicted criminal' alone.",
"page": 6
},
{
"text": "The most profound impact of the counter-narrative... was on the perceptions of individuals with criminal records (H1B): exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those presented with the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = 0.83, SE = 0.041).",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly increased Democrats' feelings of representation and their perception that elected officials are motivated by public service rather than self-interest.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Hypothesis 2B: Being presented with the Democratic party’s Trump advertisement alone, labeling him as a 'convicted criminal,' will significantly decrease (positive) motives of elected officials (i.e., more strongly believe that they are motivated by self-interest over serving the public), on average, relative to not being exposed to this advertisement.",
"page": 7
},
{
"text": "Both H2A and H2B were supported... significantly increased participants beliefs that elected officials’ motivations were to serve the public, versus serving their own self-interest ( β̂0 = 0.130, SE = 0.049).",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement and worsened perceptions of the quality of Democratic representation in federal government compared to seeing the advertisement alone.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Starting with H1A, exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = −0.172 SE = 0.048).",
"page": 11
},
{
"text": "Additionally, evidence supported H1D, namely that exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased quality of Democratic representation in federal government, relative to just Trump advertisement exposure ( β̂2 = −0.332, SE = 0.048).",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The negative effects of the Trump advertisement on perceptions of individuals with criminal records were more pronounced for participants who identified as system-impacted.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Specifically, for the items about possession of having a criminal record being a reflection of someone’s character, that most people with criminal records do not positively contribute to their communities... and that labeling people with records as ‘criminals’ may do more harm than good, the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging to perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.",
"page": 14
}
]
}
],
"scope_and_limits": [
{
"claim": "The study acknowledges limitations including the lack of a full factorial design (preventing isolation of the counter-narrative's effect alone), the inability to disentangle the impact of the image from the text in the advertisement, and the use of an online panel which raises concerns about AI-generated responses and social desirability bias.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "First, the ideal version of this survey experiment would have been to utilize a full factorial design... This was entirely done a result of binding budget constraints... Failing to include the counter-narrative only arm limits the ability to interpret the additional impact of including it with the advertisements",
"page": 16
},
{
"text": "Additionally, as the advertisement... was presented consistently across treatment arms, it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed",
"page": 16
},
{
"text": "It is also incredibly important to acknowledge that this survey was fielded amongst an online panel of survey respondents, and the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within the survey.",
"page": 16
},
{
"text": "First, it could be the case that participants were subject to the Hawthorne effect, resulting in outcomes being driven – in some capacity – by social desirability bias.",
"page": 17
}
]
}
],
"theoretical_positioning": [
{
"claim": "The paper builds upon social categorization theory to explain how salient labels applied to a group exemplar (Trump) can spill over to affect perceptions of the broader group (individuals with criminal records), while also incorporating literature on perspective-getting as a mechanism to counteract this stigma.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Specifically, social categorization theory articulates the implications and process of how individuals come to assign and distinguish individuals into groups... once the label of ‘felon’ and ‘convicted criminal’ was applied to Donald J. Trump – and done so quite routinely and saliently – theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions from Trump",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "Additionally, in a not-so-distant past... Thus, sharing political messaging highlighting dedicated, system-impacted representatives that may provide both their perspective (i.e., encouraging empathy, positive emotions, and understanding) as well as one that signals their policy alignment... would be well-suited to addressing both the affective and policy representation avenues for bolstering support.",
"page": 3
}
]
}
],
"interlocutor_claim": [
{
"claim": "The paper disputes the assumption that using stigmatizing labels like 'convicted criminal' in political advertising is a necessary or effective strategy, noting that prior evidence suggests negative ads are less effective for Democrats and that such labels worsen public perceptions of individuals with records.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Though acknowledgement of Trump’s convictions by the Democratic Party was likely unavoidable and in fact, necessary, the use of stigmatizing terms was not.",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "The more negative angle for campaign advertisements and rhetoric is important to note: prior evidence on the effects of negative campaign advertisements suggests that they tend to be utilized less by those leading in the polls... and that Democrats tend to respond more to positive ads, compared to Republicans [6]. Additionally, meta-analytic evidence does not empirically support the use of negative campaign advertisements to aid election success [7].",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "Extant literature highlights the causal impact of propagation of labels, such as ‘criminal’, to significantly worsen perceptions of individuals with records, relative to more humanizing, person-first language, such as ‘a person with a criminal record’‌‌ [8–10].",
"page": 2
}
]
}
]
}
